{"claim_parts": ["This work uses Back spread Through Time for Text Classification (BPT3C)", "a document is divided into fixed-length batches", "gradients are back-propagated to batches"],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "we propose BPT3C"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "We divide the document into fixed-length batches of size b."}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "gradients are back-propagated to the batches whose hidden states contributed to the final prediction"}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 3, "text": "In practice the method uses variable-length backpropagation sequences, not only the fixed-length batches the claim describes.", "quote": "In practice, we use variable length backpropagation sequences Merity et al. (2017a)."}]}

{"claim_parts": ["YOLOv3 struggles more with medium and larger size objects", "This medium and larger object performance is worse than before (than earlier YOLO versions)", "YOLOv3 is more successful for smaller objects"],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "However, it has comparatively worse performance on medium and larger size objects."}, {"part": 2, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "However, it has comparatively worse performance on medium and larger size objects."}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "With the new multi-scale predictions we see YOLOv3 has relatively high AP{}_{S} performance."}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "However, now we see a reversal in that trend."}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 1, "text": "The source's 'comparatively worse' compares medium and larger object performance with YOLOv3's own small-object performance; it does not state a baseline of earlier YOLO versions.", "quote": "it has comparatively worse performance on medium and larger size objects"}, {"part": 2, "text": "The source gives no earlier-version baseline for medium and larger objects, so the 'than before' comparison is not stated.", "quote": "comparatively worse performance"}, {"part": 3, "text": "The source attributes the relatively high small-object AP to the new multi-scale predictions, a condition the claim omits.", "quote": "With the new multi-scale predictions we see YOLOv3 has relatively high AP{}_{S} performance."}]}

{"claim_parts": ["The action space for language modeling is equal to the vocabulary set of language models.", "Because LM vocabularies are very large (tens of thousands of tokens), the action space is also very large.", "In general, real-world locomotion can be condensed to five quantities: movement along the X, Y, or Z axes, or a linear combination thereof.", "The authors mention that typical RL problems have an action space an order of magnitude smaller, but do not explain how those action spaces are modeled or constructed."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "consists of a token from our vocabulary"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "e.g., GPT-2/3 and T5 have a vocabulary size of 50K and 32K respectively"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "orders of magnitude larger than what most discrete action space RL algorithms are designed for"}, {"part": 4, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "orders of magnitude larger than what most discrete action space RL algorithms are designed for"}],
"missing_conditions": []}

{"claim_parts": ["The authors state that the masking policy is updated every mu steps.", "The main text does not contain the exact numerical value of mu.", "The authors attribute NLPO's outperformance over PPO probably to the occasionally updated dynamic masking function.", "Therefore mu is probably not an unbounded or very large number."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "but is updated only every \\mu steps"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "but is updated only every \\mu steps"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "is because the masking policy provides an additional constraint for the current policy"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "but of the policy from \\mu iterations ago"}, {"part": 4, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "but of the policy from \\mu iterations ago"}],
"missing_conditions": []}

{"claim_parts": ["In LM optimization for an RL agent, the action set consists of only a small part of the language model's vocabulary.", "The vocabulary of typical GPT/T5 models ranges to tens of thousands of tokens.", "General RL tasks have an action space an order of magnitude smaller than the LM action space."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "contradicts", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "An action in the environment a_{t}\\in\\mathcal{A} consists of a token from our vocabulary \\mathcal{V}."}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "e.g., GPT-2/3 and T5 have a vocabulary size of 50K and 32K respectively."}, {"part": 3, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": false, "same_scope": false, "quote": "Language generation action spaces are orders of magnitude larger than what most discrete action space RL algorithms are designed for"}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 1, "text": "Top-p sampling restricts tokens in-context to a smaller set at each step, so the effective action set can be a small part of the vocabulary under that mechanism.", "quote": "NLPO accomplishes this via top-p sampling, which restricts tokens to the smallest possible set whose cumulative probability is greater than the probability parameter p"}, {"part": 3, "text": "The source compares LM action spaces with the action spaces that most discrete-action RL algorithms are designed for, not with general RL tasks, and says 'orders of magnitude' rather than one order of magnitude.", "quote": "Language generation action spaces are orders of magnitude larger than what most discrete action space RL algorithms are designed for"}]}

{"claim_parts": ["The metrics used to compare methods for finding adversarial perturbations include the average robustness of the model, estimated in a norm (2-norm or infinity-norm).", "The metrics used to compare methods for finding adversarial perturbations include the average running time needed to find the estimated minimal perturbation."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "we compute the average robustness \\hat{\\rho}_{\\text{adv}}(f)"}, {"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "\\frac{\\|\\hat{\\bm{r}}(\\bm{x})\\|_{2}}{\\|\\bm{x}\\|_{2}}"}, {"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "Table 2 reports the \\ell_{\\infty} robustness to adversarial perturbations measured by"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "We also show the running time required for each method to compute one adversarial sample"}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 2, "text": "The source reports running time for computing one adversarial sample per method, not an average running time over the test set, and does not describe it as the time to find the estimated minimal perturbation.", "quote": "We also show the running time required for each method to compute one adversarial sample"}]}

{"claim_parts": ["The DeepFool method is designed iteratively, starting from more general non-linear differentiable classifiers and ending at very simple binary classifiers.", "The effectiveness of the greedy algorithm is justified by previous work.", "The results show very small perturbations.", "The authors claim that the method is a viable method."],
 "evidence": [
  {"part": 1, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "In the binary case, it can be seen as Newton’s iterative algorithm for finding roots of a nonlinear system of equations in the underdetermined case [15]."},
  {"part": 2, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "The convergence analysis of this optimization technique can be found for example in [21]."},
  {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "we have observed in practice that our algorithm yields very small perturbations"},
  {"part": 4, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "which are believed to be good approximations of the minimal perturbation"}
 ],
 "missing_conditions": [
  {"part": 3, "text": "The greedy algorithm is not guaranteed to converge to the optimal perturbation, and the small perturbations are only observed in practice and believed to be good approximations of the minimal perturbation.", "quote": "is not guaranteed to converge to the optimal perturbation in (1)"}
 ]}

{"claim_parts": ["LCN is a pre-processing step that normalizes the input to non-uniform scene illumination, highlights edges, and decorrelates the input dimensions.", "This normalization is performed on the first three channels of an input image.", "LCN improves convergence and helps to learn category shape."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "We perform local contrast normalization (LCN) as a pre-processing step to the input"}, {"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "to correct for non-uniform scene illumination thus reducing the dynamic range"}, {"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "highlighting edges which leads the network to learn category shape"}, {"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "improves convergence as it decorrelates the input dimensions"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "LCN is performed independently for each modality, i.e., RGB is contrast normalized as a three channel input and depth as a single channel for RGBD inputs"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "improves convergence as it decorrelates the input dimensions"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "highlighting edges which leads the network to learn category shape"}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 2, "text": "LCN is applied independently per modality, not to a fixed set of first three channels: RGB is normalized as a three-channel input, and depth is normalized as a single channel for RGBD inputs.", "quote": "depth as a single channel for RGBD inputs"}]}

{"claim_parts": ["If the number of prototypes per class is fixed and greater than 1, this would require a partitioning scheme to further cluster the support points within a class.", "This has been proposed in previous works which require a separate partitioning phase that is decoupled from the weight updates.", "The paper's approach is simple to learn with ordinary gradient descent methods."],
 "evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "If the number of prototypes per class is fixed and greater than 1, then this would require a partitioning scheme to further cluster the support points within a class."}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "however both methods require a separate partitioning phase that is decoupled from the weight updates"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "while our approach is simple to learn with ordinary gradient descent methods"}],
 "missing_conditions": []}

{"claim_parts": ["There is no aligned text and only videos are used (video training data contains no aligned text).", "The authors use only public datasets, with no paired text for videos.", "Text descriptions describe image frames, which limits associating text with video phenomena; more detailed stories are left for future work.", "For all experiments, an extrapolation network (↑F) with frame skip 5 upsamples a 16-frame video to 76 frames."],
"evidence": [
{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "Note that only the videos (no aligned text) are used."},
{"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "While prior work (Hong et al., 2022; Ho et al., 2022) have collected private text-video pairs for T2V generation, we use only public datasets (and no paired text for videos)."},
{"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "As discussed earlier, our approach can not learn associations between text and phenomenon that can only be inferred in videos."},
{"part": 3, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "depicting more detailed stories, is left for future work"},
{"part": 3, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": false, "quote": "text describing images does not capture the entirety of phenomena observed in videos"},
{"part": 4, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": false, "same_scope": false, "quote": "16 frames are sampled from the original video with random fps ranging from 1 to 30"}
],
"missing_conditions": [
{"part": 1, "text": "Text-image pairs are used to train the prior and the image models; only the video-stage training lacks aligned text, so the claim's blanket statement omits this.", "quote": "We train it on paired text-image data and do not fine-tune it on videos."},
{"part": 3, "text": "The limitation applies specifically to phenomena that can only be inferred in videos, a narrower class than the claim's general 'phenomenon in video'.", "quote": "As discussed earlier, our approach can not learn associations between text and phenomenon that can only be inferred in videos."}
]}

{"claim_parts": ["Conditioning on a varying number of frames-per-second enables an additional augmentation method that addresses the limited volume of available videos at training time.", "Conditioning on a varying number of frames-per-second provides additional control on the generated video at inference time.", "In human evaluation, raters choose this method for more realistic motion more than half of the time.", "This method excels when there are large differences between frames, where real-world knowledge of how objects move is crucial."],
"evidence": [{"part": 1, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "Conditioning on a varying number of frames-per-second, enables an additional augmentation method to tackle the limited volume of available videos at training time"}, {"part": 2, "relation": "entails", "same_entity": true, "same_scope": true, "quote": "and provides additional control on the generated video at inference time"}, {"part": 3, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": false, "same_scope": true, "quote": "Raters choose our method for more realistic motion 62% of the time on our evaluation set and 54% of the time on DrawBench."}, {"part": 4, "relation": "insufficient", "same_entity": false, "same_scope": true, "quote": "We observe that our method excels when there are large differences between frames where having real-world knowledge of how objects move is crucial."}],
"missing_conditions": [{"part": 3, "text": "The human evaluation concerns the frame interpolation network (upsampling from 1 FPS to 4 FPS) compared against FILM, not the frames-per-second conditioning method, and is limited to two evaluation sets.", "quote": "We first generate low frame rate videos (1 FPS) from text prompts in DrawBench and our evaluation set, then use each method to upsample to 4 FPS."}, {"part": 4, "text": "The observation is about the frame interpolation network in comparison with FILM on upsampling from 1 FPS to 4 FPS, not the frames-per-second conditioning method.", "quote": "We first generate low frame rate videos (1 FPS) from text prompts in DrawBench and our evaluation set, then use each method to upsample to 4 FPS."}]}

